spec:
  grid: 18-20 rows x 18-20 cols (square)
  colors: 3 distinct — bg (most common), fg (sprite), ctrl (ray/border marker); always {1,2,7} [observed]
  ctrl_pixels:
    count: 1 or 2, each on a distinct grid edge (top/bottom/left/right)
    placement: on the border row/col; when 2, they are on perpendicular edges (one vertical split, one horizontal)
    split_line: each ctrl pixel defines a cut — vertical (top/bottom edge) or horizontal (left/right edge)
  sprite:
    color: fg, single connected component, no symmetry required
    bounding_box: 5-8 rows x 8-10 cols, density 0.39-0.60
    position: roughly centered; margin >= 4 cells from every grid edge
    split_intersection: each split line passes through the sprite interior (fraction 0.4-0.6 along the bb span); 2-4 fg pixels sit on each split line
  ray: drawn from ctrl pixel inward along its row/col, painting bg cells with ctrl color, stopping at the farthest fg pixel (intermittent — skips fg cells on the line)
  transformation:
    - ray partitions sprite into 2 regions (1 ctrl) or 4 quadrants (2 ctrl)
    - fg pixels on split line(s) stay in place
    - each region touching a ctrl-pixel edge slides to its corresponding corner (piece bb flush with corner)
    - regions not touching any ctrl-pixel edge vanish (only possible with 2 ctrl pixels)
  fitting_constraint: each piece's bb width < distance from split col to its target side edge; analogous for height

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    1. Choose grid size H=W in 18-20 and pick 3 distinct colors for bg, fg, ctrl.
    2. Choose 1 or 2 ctrl pixels on distinct edges; derive c_split and/or r_split (near grid center, within 40-60% of grid width/height).
    3. For each kept region (2 or 3), generate a small irregular connected piece (3-9 fg pixels, bb 2-5 rows x 3-5 cols). Place each piece flush in its target corner, ensuring it stays strictly on its side of the split line(s).
    4. Generate 2-4 fg pixels on each split line within the sprite's eventual row/col range (these stay in place).
    5. Build the output grid — place corner pieces, split-line fg, and draw intermittent ctrl rays from edge to farthest fg on each split line.
    6. Derive input — move each corner piece back toward center so all pieces + split-line fg form one connected sprite. Place ctrl pixel(s) on border. Erase ray. Verify sprite is a single connected component and has margin >= 4 from all edges.
    7. If 2 ctrl pixels, also generate a vanishing-region piece in the remaining quadrant adjacent to the sprite (present in input only).
```
